   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "When the object comes first",
     "texto": [
      "Spanish word order is flexible, but there is a price. If you put the object before the verb, you must repeat it with a pronoun.\n\n- Vi **a Juan** ayer. → **A Juan lo** vi ayer.\n- Compré **las entradas** por internet. → **Las entradas las** compré por internet.\n- Ya sé **eso**. → **Eso** ya **lo** sé.\n- Di el libro **a tu hermana**. → **A tu hermana le** di el libro.\n\nWhy do it? To start from what you are already talking about: **¿Y el coche? El coche lo** tiene mi hermano.\n\n**Indirect objects** are different: the pronoun usually appears even when nothing has moved. **Le** di el libro **a tu hermana** is more natural than «di el libro a tu hermana».\n\n**Stressed pronouns never go alone.** **A mí me** gusta. **A ella la** llamaron. **A nosotros** no **nos** dijeron nada. You cannot say «a ella llamaron».\n\n**New information goes last.** That is why the subject often follows the verb: **Ha llegado una carta. Me lo dijo tu madre. Llamó Pedro.**"
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Things that happen by themselves",
     "texto": [
      "**Something happens with nobody to blame.** With **se**, an action has no author: **la puerta se abrió**; **el vaso se rompió**; **la luz se apagó**; **el coche se estropeó**.\n\nAdd a second pronoun and you say whom it happened to, still without blame: **se me rompió** el vaso; **se te ha caído** un guante; **se nos estropeó** el coche; **se le perdieron** las llaves.\n\nCompare: **Rompí** el vaso (I did it) and **Se me rompió** el vaso (it happened to me). **Lo hice a propósito** against **fue sin querer**.\n\nThe verb agrees with the thing: se me **perdió** la llave; se me **perdieron** las llaves.\n\n**Le vi or lo vi?** The direct object pronouns are **lo** and **la**. In much of Spain, however, people say **le** for a man: **A Juan le vi ayer**. This is called **leísmo**, and for a single male person the Academy accepts it. It is not accepted for things (el libro **lo** compré, never «le compré»). If in doubt, use **lo** and **la**: they are always correct."
     ],
